\project project_dissertation

\starttitle[title={Abstract}]
Unbiased single cell transcriptome data obtained by RNA sequencing (scRNA-Seq) allows for unprecedented research opportunities for diseases and development.
Experimental capabilities, data size, and analysis tools are constantly growing since its inception.
Due to the this combinatoric explosion, arriving at a set of best practices involves both a lot of research and implementation effort.
This work describes the author’s advances towards a consolidated and accessible scRNA-Seq analysis and visualization experience.
It focuses on the development of reusable analysis building blocks and algorithms for dimensional reduction.

In the course of this work, a general scRNA-Seq analysis workflow is explained together with the challenges and opportunities related to each step.
The author’s contributions are placed into the context of this workflow:
\n{AnnData} is a generic Python data structure for annotated matrices such as scRNA-Seq expressions.
It is used by \n{Scanpy}, an efficient Python framework with building blocks for preprocessing, analysis, and visualization of scRNA-Seq data.
\n{Destiny} is an efficient implementation of the diffusion map algorithm for R optimized for biological data.
It contains the gene relevance algorithm for detecting genes that drive a given dimensional reduction.
BART-Seq is an inexpensive protocol for targeted transcriptome quantification for large amounts of samples.
\stoptitle

